d045030201d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115792" :question/problem "Solve for $w$ where $1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}=\\frac{w+5}{3w}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $w=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $3w,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =\\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w\\cdot (1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =3w\\cdot (\\frac{w+5}{3w}) \\\\ 3w\\cdot 1-3w\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =3w\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w-\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{\\cancel{3w}} & =\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{\\cancel{3w}} \\\\ 3w-(2w-3) & =w+5 \\\\ 3w-2w+3 & =w+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $w,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-2w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w-w & =5-3 \\\\ 0 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115792/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115792"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75924/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" :answer/id #uuid "a0852078-bd2f-5fc7-8d18-4ec9aef27881" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75924/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b11536c1-f160-5dde-9000-a3d6c82b4e14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75924/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9d26596a-2580-56b9-a51e-091e15a6f83f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75924/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "4babc81f-f4aa-5087-9ccb-6441e77c0276" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75924/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "a7b51866-93bb-5ebe-8dcd-e9606bcd78cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75924/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d892d7e1-af0a-57bb-825f-7b02d3ee66df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75924/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-75924/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" "q-75924/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-75924/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-75924/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-75924/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75924" :question/id #uuid "d52ee725-03cf-5282-8779-c17b6cfc82ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-75924" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x} & =4 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}) & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{x}-x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{x} & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{\\cancel{x}}-\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{x}} & =4x \\\\ x+7-(1-x) & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7-(1-x) & =4x \\\\ 2x+6 & =4x \\\\ 4x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=3\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75924/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75924"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115788/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "24a4aeee-88ab-5aff-8e3e-50694ddbc401" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115788/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "45934fab-b7ef-54c6-892c-926d34e2d828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115788/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "991aec93-e276-545d-8913-e0550aceb660" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115788/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "37035c74-5b01-5f88-be92-14119e86d022" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115788/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "807e495c-435d-51b8-9f54-bae8720e3280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115788/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0a317f28-d321-56f8-8d64-6f2a99feb03e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115788/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-115788/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-115788/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-115788/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-115788/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-115788/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115788" :question/id #uuid "ffcdf275-e056-5057-8270-11fc9a6292de" :question/math-academy-id "q-115788" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}=\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $m=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by ${m}^{3},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}) & ={m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}) \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+{m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & ={m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ \\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}}+\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} & =\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} \\\\ 3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m+1 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m-2m & =3-1 \\\\ 2m & =2 \\\\ m & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=1\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115788/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115788"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75925/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" :answer/id #uuid "1255a35d-2301-567e-bb86-6c63ed06fe17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75925/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "26731867-2d19-5518-8179-43188619c7e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75925/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "6227840d-47aa-5aae-ac1b-f5709b35a958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75925/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "89518ee2-acbd-5c46-ae24-725a8dd5f769" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75925/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "4b7ec68e-ec1a-53d6-aeb9-2d58ccf6990e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75925/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "657e5281-20e8-55fb-8169-6fa747df3c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75925/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" :answer-field/choices ["q-75925/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" "q-75925/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-75925/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-75925/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-75925/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75925" :question/id #uuid "6763e3f1-b5fe-59b4-92c1-e11e65c136d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75925" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{5x}=\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $5x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{5x} & =\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5 \\\\ 5x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{5x}) & =5x\\cdot (\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5) \\\\ 5x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{5x} & =5x\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5x\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{5x}} & =\\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{\\cancel{5x}}+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3-20x+25x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-20x+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3+5x \\\\ 5x-x & =-2-3 \\\\ 4x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{5}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75925/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75925"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115791/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "9bf1a9e4-97fd-50c0-9b8c-4cc59a485297" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115791/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "778881e4-b5f1-546a-90b6-30cbad641ce0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115791/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "4c9e733e-fe10-582c-9f6a-6867ab241d20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115791/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" :answer/id #uuid "95d562e6-c1ad-52c0-806f-0d6089d60b30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115791/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "701ef8e2-c225-5250-9bbf-3424b2f290db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115791/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "64cddbcd-5541-5889-a231-e5fecc8e2564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115791/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115791/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-115791/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-115791/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-115791/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" "q-115791/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115791" :question/id #uuid "c7e93f25-c681-5303-8b0f-eaffed982cfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-115791" :question/problem "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{z+1}{2z}=2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $z=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ 2z\\cdot (\\frac{z+1}{2z}) & =2z\\cdot (2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}) \\\\ 2z\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2z\\cdot 2-2z\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ \\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} & =2z\\cdot 2-\\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ z+1 & =4z-(2z+1) \\\\ z+1 & =4z-2z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =4z-2z-1 \\\\ z+1 & =2z-1 \\\\ -z & =-2 \\\\ z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115791/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115791"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276753/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "0dc9a3de-0b24-5c5e-87d7-9385cc3caab7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276753/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "38b14b68-7519-54ca-bc57-ece0d8915b86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276753/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-276753/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276753" :question/id #uuid "d0cdfc02-343a-5c3a-9cb0-b6bccd8d23e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-276753" :question/problem "Solve for $p$ where $\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p}=-\\frac{p-2}{6p}.$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $p=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $p$ and $6p.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(p,6p)=6p.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6p,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p} & =-\\frac{p-2}{6p} \\\\ 6p\\cdot (\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p}) & =6p\\cdot (-\\frac{p-2}{6p}) \\\\ 6p\\cdot \\frac{p+6}{6p}-6\\cdot p\\cdot \\frac{2}{p} & =6p(-\\frac{p-2}{6p}) \\\\ \\cancel{6p}\\cdot \\frac{p+6}{\\cancel{6p}}-6\\cdot \\cancel{p}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{p}} & =\\cancel{6p}(-\\frac{p-2}{\\cancel{6p}}) \\\\ p+6-12 & =-(p-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $p,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+6-12 & =-(p-2) \\\\ p-6 & =-p+2 \\\\ 2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $p=0$ as a solution. However, since $p=4\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $p=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-276753/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276753"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14738/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "42429924-3dcc-586f-bf43-ad3d467e106f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14738/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" :answer/id #uuid "d90ea437-2007-503f-b84f-874863857d5c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14738/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "4b1cbd5b-ec35-5a99-b7e4-8e884d54ba60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14738/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "9fb70c25-4b71-5d88-b849-1b7eb695bff9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14738/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1bc1825c-ab48-5f31-9d7e-0d13ce0bab89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14738/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7de79927-60a3-522c-86e1-b95342c3bbd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-14738/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" :answer-field/choices ["q-14738/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-14738/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" "q-14738/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-14738/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-14738/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14738" :question/id #uuid "26c39016-3ce4-5951-826f-432be0bef6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-14738" :question/problem "Solve for $p$ where $\\frac{1}{p}=\\frac{10}{9p}+\\frac{p-1}{9p}.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $p=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $p$ and $9p.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(p,9p)=9p.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $9p,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{p} & =\\frac{10}{9p}+\\frac{p-1}{9p} \\\\ 9p\\cdot (\\frac{1}{p}) & =9p\\cdot (\\frac{10}{9p}+\\frac{p-1}{9p}) \\\\ 9p\\cdot \\frac{1}{p} & =9p\\cdot \\frac{10}{9p}+9p\\cdot \\frac{p-1}{9p} \\\\ 9\\cancel{p}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{p}} & =\\cancel{9p}\\cdot \\frac{10}{\\cancel{9p}}+\\cancel{9p}\\cdot \\frac{p-1}{\\cancel{9p}} \\\\ 9\\cdot (1) & =10+p-1 \\\\ 9 & =10+p-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $p,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =10+p-1 \\\\ 9 & =p+9 \\\\ p & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $p=0$ as a solution. Since $p=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-14738/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14738"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75930/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "bf7b39b5-3055-5e51-99a2-02eaae25a532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75930/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" :answer/id #uuid "0bf1dc2a-3da0-584c-bdbc-c51eb1ec995d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75930/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "ad93d612-7a2c-549e-ab1d-4eb261ef6066" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75930/selection/ab22b83e290700d204faf1095cfc25a4b648955dede5993efb51bc6a54cd3128" :answer/id #uuid "bba77d5b-80fb-5c53-bb0e-c975d4a8d1be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75930/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "c5f8c6d4-e6f8-5e78-b0a9-39f916a0e5a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75930/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "31fa9c63-ec5b-5569-b39c-0fa14119d094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75930/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer-field/choices ["q-75930/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-75930/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" "q-75930/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-75930/selection/ab22b83e290700d204faf1095cfc25a4b648955dede5993efb51bc6a54cd3128" "q-75930/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75930" :question/id #uuid "d26dc9c9-dbd4-5fbf-a50c-dd51b95cbdb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75930" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\frac{a+3}{3a}=\\frac{a-1}{a}+2.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $a=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $a$ and $3a.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a,3a)=3a.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3a,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+3}{3a} & =\\frac{a-1}{a}+2 \\\\ 3a\\cdot (\\frac{a+3}{3a}) & =3a\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{a}+2) \\\\ 3a\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{3a} & =3a\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{a}+3a\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{\\cancel{3a}} & =3\\cancel{a}\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{a}}+6a \\\\ a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a \\\\ a+3 & =9a-3 \\\\ 9a-a & =3+3 \\\\ 8a & =6 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. But since $a=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75930/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75930"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115794/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "106304cd-7d4d-54f9-b6ac-ae99227f5ffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115794/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" :answer/id #uuid "89c014a0-0c9b-5552-a18d-044d0784a040" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115794/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "5b320f40-9600-53a1-97f1-49c94f0cfcd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115794/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "f4a664bf-ff72-5eef-8f99-d29cd972e162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115794/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "32dc52ed-b5e1-59d7-9ea7-2652d96a2234" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115794/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "50006c08-45ae-52df-a09e-7c19aa9370fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115794/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer-field/choices ["q-115794/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-115794/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" "q-115794/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-115794/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-115794/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115794" :question/id #uuid "2691c2ad-adb0-5c66-a320-3594e74b4de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115794" :question/problem "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $z=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3z$ and $6z.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3z,6z)=6z.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z} \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z})+6z\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ \\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3z}\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{\\cancel{3z}})+\\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) \\\\ 2z-1 & =2\\cdot (3z-1)+z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-1 & =2(3z-1)+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =6z-2+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =7z-3 \\\\ 2z-7z & =-3+1 \\\\ -5z & =-2 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=\\frac{2}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115794/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115794"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276759/field-1/79d4e6b302dbde2f8f3ad92196c7aebc2519ad72cc6074bdf2ac2d7a919c20b6" :answer/id #uuid "5374fbd3-7682-5ae7-9345-492ce2ab92a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276759/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e4fb54f-ec02-5f99-b883-d4d7d6a8da3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276759/field-1/79d4e6b302dbde2f8f3ad92196c7aebc2519ad72cc6074bdf2ac2d7a919c20b6" :answer-field/choices ["q-276759/field-1/79d4e6b302dbde2f8f3ad92196c7aebc2519ad72cc6074bdf2ac2d7a919c20b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276759" :question/id #uuid "6ad564dd-c274-5195-a2e9-7fe676b2cd5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-276759" :question/problem "Solve for $d$ where $\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d}=1.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $d=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3d$ and $4d.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3d,4d)=12d.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12d,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =1 \\\\ 12d(\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d}) & =12d\\cdot 1 \\\\ 12d\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{3d}+12d\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =12d \\\\ 4\\cdot 3d\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{3d}+3\\cdot 4d\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =12d \\\\ 4\\cdot \\cancel{3d}\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{\\cancel{3d}}+3\\cdot \\cancel{4d}\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{\\cancel{4d}} & =12d \\\\ 4(10-d)+3(3d-5) & =12d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $d,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(10-d)+3(3d-5) & =12d \\\\ 40-4d+9d-15 & =12d \\\\ 25+5d & =12d \\\\ -7d & =-25 \\\\ d & =\\frac{25}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $d=0$ as a solution. However, since $d=\\frac{25}{7}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $d=\\frac{25}{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-276759/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276759"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-75585"] :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x,2x,$ and $4x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,2x,4x)=4x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x} \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =2\\cdot 2x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{\\cancel{2x}})+\\cancel{4x}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{4x}}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (1) & =2\\cdot (2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =2(2+x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2(2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =4+2x+3 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{3}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-276856/field-1/7271e6103236c35d7eb168d301bb4ff5916fa3243e6da1cc695191718f154698" :answer/id #uuid "7b65ee2c-7343-58ab-bc69-3a91858cc617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{39}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276856/field-1/9fc7c9ee04032ebc8e2c1778ace8d3b3c72e61e1a82f73d8e6a40bae96d52b58" :answer/id #uuid "b395abe8-856b-5d31-a201-bde2195ab2e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276856/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc88da31-e59a-5ea4-b2b0-ce546158baed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276856/field-1/7271e6103236c35d7eb168d301bb4ff5916fa3243e6da1cc695191718f154698" :answer-field/choices ["q-276856/field-1/7271e6103236c35d7eb168d301bb4ff5916fa3243e6da1cc695191718f154698" "q-276856/field-1/9fc7c9ee04032ebc8e2c1778ace8d3b3c72e61e1a82f73d8e6a40bae96d52b58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276856" :question/id #uuid "d526a2bd-2970-5091-a158-ab47d03ea9b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-276856" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m}=\\frac{5}{12m}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $m,\\,6m,$ and $12m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,6m,12m)=12m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m} & =\\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12m(\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m}) & =12m(\\frac{5}{12m}) \\\\ 12m\\cdot \\frac{3}{m}+12m\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{6m} & =12m\\cdot \\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12\\cdot m\\cdot \\frac{3}{m}+2\\cdot 6m\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{6m} & =12m\\cdot \\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}}+2\\cdot \\cancel{6m}\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{\\cancel{6m}} & =\\cancel{12m}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{12m}} \\\\ 36+2(4+m) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36+2(4+m) & =5 \\\\ 36+8+2m & =5 \\\\ 44+2m & =5 \\\\ 2m & =-39 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{39}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. However, since $m=-\\frac{39}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-\\frac{39}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-276856/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276856"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276864/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "6c4d20f3-8ad1-5f11-baa5-0706c3e43145" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276864/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d9bd73a-df28-55f0-b36f-6ceb0bea272e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276864/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer-field/choices ["q-276864/field-1/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276864" :question/id #uuid "1c0e0aa0-574c-5bf8-9e16-b95a361eaa01" :question/math-academy-id "q-276864" :question/problem "Solve for $f$ where $\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}=\\frac{f+2}{2f}.$\n\n$f=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $f=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $f,\\,2f,$ and $5f.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(f,2f,5f)=10f.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10f,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =\\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10f(\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}) & =10f(\\frac{f+2}{2f}) \\\\ 10f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-10f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =10f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-2\\cdot 5f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =5\\cdot 2f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{f}\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{\\cancel{f}}-2\\cdot \\cancel{5f}\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{\\cancel{5f}} & =5\\cdot \\cancel{2f}\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{\\cancel{2f}} \\\\ 10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $f,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2) \\\\ 10f+60-6f-18 & =5f+10 \\\\ 4f+42 & =5f+10 \\\\ -f & =-32 \\\\ f & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $f=0$ as a solution. But since $f=32\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $f=32.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-276864/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276864"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75941/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "25e96631-7883-5a31-8f6f-42f229fb8480" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75941/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "3ea4a7e0-609c-59fa-9b35-6dc8a458f79b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75941/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "5f96555d-ca2f-5aa4-8db2-1aaed9d8536f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75941/selection/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" :answer/id #uuid "6e87fa72-a14e-5be9-93d6-4dded0403756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75941/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "7d2aa16b-599c-54ea-ae2e-f250ef67ad04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75941/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f999c9f2-c51c-5624-92bc-e401a0332ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75941/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-75941/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-75941/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-75941/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-75941/selection/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" "q-75941/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75941" :question/id #uuid "bdaba5d6-75ab-535e-a5ea-fa0b29055b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-75941" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}=\\frac{m+4}{15m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,5m,$ and $15m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,5m,15m)=15m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $15m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m} & =\\frac{m+4}{15m} \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+3\\cdot 5m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{\\cancel{3m}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{5m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{\\cancel{5m}}) & =\\cancel{15m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{\\cancel{15m}}) \\\\ 5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4 \\\\ 10m-15+3m+15 & =m+4 \\\\ 13m & =m+4 \\\\ 12m & =4 \\\\ m & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75941/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75941"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276861/field-1/8c7e8abb1bf309b915c30abc6990f0d3a287c31dedca86ccd0b3b7d169109549" :answer/id #uuid "e2ce1a6b-841f-56b8-8a37-b0170f64dd1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{47}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276861/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b5e08b-21a8-5fd3-b71b-d818bc1dcf06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276861/field-1/8c7e8abb1bf309b915c30abc6990f0d3a287c31dedca86ccd0b3b7d169109549" :answer-field/choices ["q-276861/field-1/8c7e8abb1bf309b915c30abc6990f0d3a287c31dedca86ccd0b3b7d169109549"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276861" :question/id #uuid "7a66a266-1f4f-5088-8c13-0c5dd6ede389" :question/math-academy-id "q-276861" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a}=\\frac{7}{10a}.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $a=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $a,\\,5a,$ and $10a.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a,5a,10a)=10a.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10a,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a} & =\\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10a(\\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a}) & =10a(\\frac{7}{10a}) \\\\ 10a\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{a}+10a\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{5a} & =10a\\cdot \\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10\\cdot a\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{a}+2\\cdot 5a\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{5a} & =10a\\cdot \\frac{7}{10a} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot \\frac{a+5}{\\cancel{a}}+2\\cdot \\cancel{5a}\\cdot \\frac{2-a}{\\cancel{5a}} & =\\cancel{10a}\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{10a}} \\\\ 10(a+5)+2(2-a) & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(a+5)+2(2-a) & =7 \\\\ 10a+50+4-2a & =7 \\\\ 8a+54 & =7 \\\\ 8a & =-47 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{47}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. However, since $a=-\\frac{47}{8}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $a=-\\frac{47}{8}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-276861/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276861"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276866/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "2968da21-01d3-5325-9995-fdcf3d1b7461" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276866/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3fcfdf-1707-5364-918b-b99de84e5d69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276866/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-276866/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276866" :question/id #uuid "2c9f093a-53ef-57b3-b449-91ed1f4936c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-276866" :question/problem "Solve for $y$ where $\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y}=\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $4y,\\,{y}^{2},$ and $2{y}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4y,{y}^{2},2{y}^{2})=4{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4{y}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y} & =\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}(\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y}) & =4{y}^{2}(\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4y} & =4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-4y\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{5}{4y} & =2\\cdot 2{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}-\\cancel{4y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4y}} & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\cancel{2{y}^{2}}} \\\\ 4(y+2)-y\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $y,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(y+2)-y\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\ 4y+8-5y & =6 \\\\ -y+8 & =6 \\\\ -y & =-2 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $y=0$ as a solution. But since $y=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $y=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-276866/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276866"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116104/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "b90c4c87-903d-5a2f-a7c3-1015dcd1ef2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116104/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "c866b06a-07cb-5476-88bc-cb808a7634d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116104/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "861ae8f3-9b2f-5572-aaaa-d3a2e774ecde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116104/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" :answer/id #uuid "7541e7d0-ad51-5c0f-ab13-2931587dfca6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116104/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "3a8ae60c-e97e-5586-92b5-61d827515f10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116104/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "71c79f5f-fc6d-56d2-a05d-e1f333770750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116104/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-116104/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-116104/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-116104/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-116104/selection/6d6811ceb4fb69a54ce1b24e6804e5f0148bafa1b76b847b06436eb966d36b67" "q-116104/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116104" :question/id #uuid "e0d0ed16-601f-5cbf-96d8-d06b2516c58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-116104" :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and ${x}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,{x}^{2})={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x})-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}})-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\ (3x-1) & =x\\cdot 2-(x-3) \\\\ (3x-1) & =2x-(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1) & =2x-(x-3) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x-x+3 \\\\ 3x-1 & =x+3 \\\\ 3x-x & =3+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-116104/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116104"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116106/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "097e3de9-2c1f-59f1-a71c-e56fa91cb910" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116106/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "a98d933b-93a8-561c-b7cd-8d54d7403981" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116106/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "9275948a-21cf-59b2-80a8-f8bb12b2dc10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116106/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "4ba5489d-308e-5e32-b794-76dfb67900d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116106/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "fe699b7b-dd5f-559e-aa77-5db5f3f8f534" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116106/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5b4897-c635-5f74-b579-fbbe30a7bb36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116106/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-116106/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" "q-116106/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-116106/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-116106/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-116106/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116106" :question/id #uuid "bd8462d2-6640-5241-b864-8f7140d8b688" :question/math-academy-id "q-116106" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2{m}^{2},$ ${m}^{2}$ and $m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,{m}^{2},2{m}^{2})=2{m}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{m}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}} \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m})-2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}})-\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{m}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot 3-(3m+4) & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6m-(3m+4) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6m-(3m+4) & =2 \\\\ 6m-3m-4 & =2 \\\\ 3m & =6 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-116106/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116106"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276874/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "c44519ee-e5ea-5b9b-9196-684753b9b513" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276874/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "fe374b5e-7bc0-5a9d-a187-eb343203d909" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276874/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9cce1b-b108-5de1-b6c5-743d552b78dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276874/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-276874/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-276874/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276874" :question/id #uuid "06513a9d-b086-5e2f-a69e-291add4c2063" :question/math-academy-id "q-276874" :question/problem "Solve for $n$ where $\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $n$ and ${n}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n,{n}^{2})={n}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${n}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{6}{n} \\\\ {n}^{2}(\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}) & ={n}^{2}(\\frac{6}{n}) \\\\ {n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+{n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & ={n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n} \\\\ {n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+{n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & =n\\cdot n\\cdot \\frac{6}{n} \\\\ \\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}}+\\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}} & =n\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ n+7+n-5 & =n\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $n,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+7+n-5 & =n\\cdot 6 \\\\ 2n+2 & =6n \\\\ -4n & =-2 \\\\ n & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $n=0$ as a solution. But since $n=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $n=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-276874/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276874"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-3547"] :question/worked-solution "We note that $b=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $36b$ and $12{b}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(36b,12{b}^{2})=36{b}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $36{b}^{2},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}} & =-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}} \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b})+36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =b\\cdot 36b\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b})+3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}) & =b\\cdot \\cancel{36b}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{\\cancel{36b}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}) \\\\ 3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $b,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8) \\\\ 15b+24 & =-12b+12b+24 \\\\ 15b+24 & =24 \\\\ 15b & =24-24 \\\\ 15b & =0 \\\\ b & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $b=0$ as a solution. Since $b=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
